{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:09:27Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 86, "kind": "tool_result", "text": "# Design: Let me undo deleting a task\n\n**Taiga:** #13\n**Spec:** `docs/spec.md`\n\n## Summary of the interaction\n\nDeleting a task no longer needs a confirmation dialog \u2014 the ticket asks for\nforgiveness, not permission. The row disappears immediately (per spec, this\nis `App.jsx` filtering it out of `tasks`, not mutating it) and a small\nnotice appears at the bottom of the viewport with the task's title and an\nUndo button. The notice disappears on its own after 5 seconds, or on click.\n\n## Components: reused vs. new\n\n- **New:** `frontend/src/components/UndoToast.jsx` \u2014 one row per pending\n  delete, per the spec's affected-areas note.\n- **Reused as-is, no changes:** `.task`/`.task-list` row styling for the\n  restored row (nothing to design \u2014 the row just re-enters the existing\n  list unchanged, including `.task.done`'s strikethrough for AC4).\n- **No new UI library, icon set, or token system.** The rest of the app is\n  plain CSS with no icon usage anywhere (checked `TaskList.jsx`,\n  `ClearCompleted.jsx`, `TaskCounts.jsx`, `TaskFilter.jsx` \u2014 the only\n  \"icon\" in the app is the inline `\u26a0` glyph on `.overdue-badge`). This\n  design follows suit: text only, no icon.\n\n## Data shape into the component\n\n`App.jsx` already holds full task objects in `tasks` (untouched during the\nundo window, per spec). `UndoToast` doesn't need its own title snapshot \u2014\nrender it as:\n\n```jsx\n<UndoToast\n  pending={pendingDeleteIds.map((id) => tasks.find((t) => t.id === id))}\n  onUndo={cancelPendingDelete}\n/>\n```\n\ni.e. `pending` is an array of full task objects (oldest pending-delete\nfirst, matching insertion order \u2014 no reordering logic needed). `UndoToast`\nonly reads `.id` and `.title` off each.\n\n## Placement in the tree and DOM\n\nMount `<UndoToast>` as the last child of `<main className=\"app\">` in\n`App.jsx`, after `<TaskList>` (unconditionally \u2014 not gated behind\n`!loading`, since a pending delete can only exist once tasks have loaded).\nIt's positioned with `position: fixed`, so DOM order doesn't affect visual\nplacement, but it does fix tab order: keyboard users reach the Undo\nbutton(s) *last*, after all task-row controls. That's an accepted\nconsequence, not a gap to design around \u2014 see \"Focus\" below.\n\n## States\n\n### Empty (no pending deletes)\n\n`UndoToast` returns `null`. Nothing is rendered \u2014 no empty container sits\nin the DOM, matching the fact that there's nothing to announce.", "meta": {"native_type": "user", "tool_use_id": "toolu_01Gy647SQQQdMExFBF9toNGV", "session_id": "654814c6-720b-4415-b6c7-fe9892cf255f"}}
