{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:12:19Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 217, "kind": "message", "text": "## Review: Taiga #13 \u2014 Let me undo deleting a task (attempt 2)\n\nVerified against `docs/spec.md`'s 6 ACs and `docs/design.md`. Confirmed the round-1 crash (`UndoToast.jsx:6` on `Clear completed` racing a pending delete) is genuinely fixed \u2014 `pending={tasks.filter((t) => pendingDeletes.has(t.id))}` naturally excludes a task once it drops out of `tasks`, so no `undefined` reaches the map. `npm run build` passes. I could not spin up a live browser repro (dev server launch needs approval this headless session doesn't have \u2014 same gap noted in the developer's handoff for `npm run lint`), so the flicker finding below is a traced-through-code finding, not a live repro; the trace is based on standard JS/React execution order and I'm confident in it, but flagging the verification gap per the \"report honestly\" convention.\n\n## Will block\n\n**`frontend/src/App.jsx:76-93`** (Alan Heitkotter \u2014 failure paths / ordering of effects). `deferDelete`'s timer callback removes the id from `pendingDeletes` **before** awaiting `api.deleteTask(id)`:\n\n```js\nconst timer = setTimeout(async () => {\n  setPendingDeletes((prev) => { ... next.delete(id); return next })  // \u2190 commits first\n  try {\n    await api.deleteTask(id)   // \u2190 network round trip happens after\n    await refresh()\n  } ...\n```\n\nSince `activeTasks` is computed as `tasks.filter((t) => !pendingDeletes.has(t.id))` (App.jsx:39), the moment this state update commits, the task is back in `activeTasks` \u2014 it reappears in the list, counts, and filters \u2014 because `tasks` itself hasn't been touched yet. Only once `refresh()` resolves (a real network round trip later) does the task disappear again. This isn't a rare race: it's the **default path**, every time a delete is left to expire naturally (the common case \u2014 no undo clicked). The user sees: toast disappears \u2192 task flashes back into the list, fully interactive (Edit/Done/Delete all live again) \u2192 task vanishes a moment later with no toast, no warning. That directly undermines AC3 (\"the DELETE request is sent... and the notice disappears on its own\") and the feature's whole premise of a clean, predictable deletion.\n\n**Fix:** don't release the `pendingDeletes` entry until the delete attempt (success or the swallowed 404) is settled \u2014 move the `setPendingDeletes` removal into a `finally` after the `try`/`catch`, not before it:\n\n```js\nconst timer = setTimeout(async () => {\n  try {\n    await api.deleteTask(id)\n    await refresh()\n  } catch (e) {\n    if (e.message !== 'no such task') setError(e.message)\n  } finally {\n    setPendingDeletes((prev) => { const next = new Map(prev); next.delete(id); return next })\n  }\n}, UNDO_WINDOW_MS)\n```\n\n## Will comment\n\n**`frontend/src/App.jsx:134`** (Eric Goto \u2014 list/collection ordering). `docs/design.md:41` and `:80` explicitly specify \"oldest pending-delete first\" stacking, and the original design's data shape (`pendingDeleteIds.map(...)`) preserved deletion order. The round-2 fix switched to `tasks.filter((t) => pendingDeletes.has(t.id))`, which orders by `tasks`' own order (task creation/list order), not deletion order. Delete two tasks out of list order and the toast stack no longer matches \"oldest deleted on top\" as documented. Not a crash, but a documented design commitment that's silently not met.\n\n**Fix:** iterate `pendingDeletes`' own key order (a `Map` preserves insertion order) and guard against a since-vanished task, keeping both the crash fix and the deletion-order guarantee:\n```js\npending={[...pendingDeletes.keys()].map((id) => tasks.find((t) => t.id === id)).filter(Boolean)}\n```\n\n## Worth considering\n\n**`frontend/src/App.jsx:96-103`** (Alan Heitkotter \u2014 purity of state updaters). `cancelPendingDelete`'s `setPendingDeletes` updater calls `clearTimeout(prev.get(id))` as a side effect inside the updater function. React (especially under `StrictMode`, which `main.jsx:6` enables) may invoke updaters more than once to surface impurities; here it's harmless since `clearTimeout` on an already-cleared/`undefined` handle is a no-op, but it's worth moving the `clearTimeout` call outside the updater (read `pendingDeletes.get(id)` from render scope, or use a ref) if this pattern gets reused elsewhere.\n\nVERDICT: changes-requested", "meta": {"native_type": "assistant", "block_type": "text", "session_id": "654814c6-720b-4415-b6c7-fe9892cf255f"}}
{"ts": "2026-08-17T13:12:19Z", "agent": "claude", "seq": 218, "kind": "status", "text": "## Review: Taiga #13 \u2014 Let me undo deleting a task (attempt 2)\n\nVerified against `docs/spec.md`'s 6 ACs and `docs/design.md`. Confirmed the round-1 crash (`UndoToast.jsx:6` on `Clear completed` racing a pending delete) is genuinely fixed \u2014 `pending={tasks.filter((t) => pendingDeletes.has(t.id))}` naturally excludes a task once it drops out of `tasks`, so no `undefined` reaches the map. `npm run build` passes. I could not spin up a live browser repro (dev server launch needs approval this headless session doesn't have \u2014 same gap noted in the developer's handoff for `npm run lint`), so the flicker finding below is a traced-through-code finding, not a live repro; the trace is based on standard JS/React execution order and I'm confident in it, but flagging the verification gap per the \"report honestly\" convention.\n\n## Will block\n\n**`frontend/src/App.jsx:76-93`** (Alan Heitkotter \u2014 failure paths / ordering of effects). `deferDelete`'s timer callback removes the id from `pendingDeletes` **before** awaiting `api.deleteTask(id)`:\n\n```js\nconst timer = setTimeout(async () => {\n  setPendingDeletes((prev) => { ... next.delete(id); return next })  // \u2190 commits first\n  try {\n    await api.deleteTask(id)   // \u2190 network round trip happens after\n    await refresh()\n  } ...\n```\n\nSince `activeTasks` is computed as `tasks.filter((t) => !pendingDeletes.has(t.id))` (App.jsx:39), the moment this state update commits, the task is back in `activeTasks` \u2014 it reappears in the list, counts, and filters \u2014 because `tasks` itself hasn't been touched yet. Only once `refresh()` resolves (a real network round trip later) does the task disappear again. This isn't a rare race: it's the **default path**, every time a delete is left to expire naturally (the common case \u2014 no undo clicked). The user sees: toast disappears \u2192 task flashes back into the